== Probe 1: server behaviour for a never-existing near-int64-max id ==
GET  /pet/9223372036854565001 -> 200 {"id":9223372036854565001,"category":{"id":0,"name":"string"},"name":"doggie","photoUrls":["string"],"tags":[{"id":0,"name":"string"}],"status":"string"}

== Probe 2: DELETE works correctly when the exact id is used ==
POST /pet -> 200, exact id from server = 9223372036854565253 (digits: 19)
DELETE /pet/<exact id> -> 200 {"code":200,"type":"unknown","message":"9223372036854565253"}
GET  /pet/<exact id> after delete -> 404 {"code":1,"type":"error","message":"Pet not found"}

== Reference: JS Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER = 9007199254740991 (16 digits) ==
==            pet ids issued by API   = 9223372036854565253 (19 digits) - NOT JS-safe ==

== Summary of precision issue observed during the run ==
TC-DEL-00 created pet id .......65121 (exact, read via Python)
TC-DEL-01 requested delete of ..65000 (16 significant digits - Postman/JS mangled the id)
=> the collection's DELETE did not target the pet it created; id ..65000 was a pre-existing record

